"Compositional Planning with Jumpy World Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19634)\nciteturn16academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: Compositional Planning with Jumpy World Models ... Motivated by the geometric policy composition framework introduced in arXiv:2206.08736, we address these challenges by learning predictive models of multi-step dynamics -- so-called jumpy world models -- that capture state occupancies induced by pre-trained policies across multiple timescales in an off-policy manner.Building on Temporal Difference Flows (arXiv:2503.09817), we enhance these models with a novel consistency objective that aligns predictions across timescales, improving long-horizon predictive accuracy.\nTitle: Compositional Planning with Jumpy World Models\nAuthors: Jesse Farebrother, Matteo Pirotta, Andrea Tirinzoni, Marc G. Bellemare, Alessandro Lazaric, Ahmed Touati\nDate: Mon Feb 23 09:22:21 2026\n\nThe ability to plan with temporal abstractions is central to intelligent decision-making. Rather than reasoning over primitive actions, we study agents that compose pre-trained policies as temporally extended actions, enabling solutions to complex tasks that no constituent alone can solve. Such compositional planning remains elusive as compounding errors in long-horizon predictions make it challenging to estimate the visitation distribution induced by sequencing policies. Motivated by the geometric policy composition framework introduced in arXiv:2206.08736, we address these challenges by learning predictive models of multi-step dynamics -- so-called jumpy world models -- that capture state occupancies induced by pre-trained policies across multiple timescales in an off-policy manner. Building on Temporal Difference Flows (arXiv:2503.09817), we enhance these models with a novel consistency objective that aligns predictions across timescales, improving long-horizon predictive accuracy. We further demonstrate how to combine these generative predictions to estimate the value of executing arbitrary sequences of policies over varying timescales. Empirically, we find that compositional planning with jumpy world models significantly improves zero-shot performance across a wide range of base policies on challenging manipulation and navigation tasks, yielding, on average, a 200% relative improvement over planning with primitive actions on long-horizon tasks.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgress Reward Modeling for Robotic Learning: A Comprehensive Survey · ArXivSignals (https://arxivsignals.io/papers/2607.21655)\nciteturn16search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; This survey organizes progress-reward research along three connected views: the model interface, the construction mechanism, and the data and benchmarks behind it. ... This timeline shows how progress-reward work has expanded from demonstration and visual-distance signals to preference learning, language-conditioned rewards, foundation-model scoring, and explicit temporal reasoning. ... Structured goals are precise and compositional when APIs expose the right state, but that access often disappears in the real world. ... Progress-fidelity tests include scalar calibration against percentages, rubric scores, stages, or hop values; temporal consistency through ordering, rank correlation, or monotonicity; relative progress through pairwise preferences; task grounding under mismatched instructions or counterfactual goals; and answerability or uncertainty when history, viewpoints, or goals are insufficient. ... They are a chain from task evidence to constructed signal to validated use—and every link must be explicit before a robot can safely learn from it.\nA terminal success signal compresses all of that into one bit: useful for judging the destination, but nearly silent about the route.\n\nProgress rewards fill in that missing middle. They describe how an execution is changing over time, helping a learner assign credit to actions, distinguish useful motion from irrelevant motion, notice stagnation or regression, and detect failure before the final outcome. The same signal can serve as a reward, an online monitor, a trajectory filter, a reranking score, or a planning aid.\n\nThe difficulty is that progress is not directly visible in one image. It is task-conditioned and often history-dependent: an identical-looking state can mean different things after different completed subgoals. A useful model must infer the latent stage of an evolving task, not merely recognize a familiar scene.\n\nImage: Fig. 1\n\nFig. 1. This timeline shows how progress-reward work has expanded from demonstration and visual-distance signals to preference learning, language-conditioned rewards, foundation-model scoring, and explicit temporal reasoning. The spread of approaches motivates a shared vocabulary before results can be compared.\n\nThe survey question\n\nWhen a paper says its model measures progress, what information does it use, what exactly does it output, and what evidence supports that interpretation? A downstream policy improvement is valuable, but it is not automatically proof that the underlying score is calibrated or causally faithful.\n\nThe gap\n\n## Similar names hide different problems\n\nThat ambiguity is why the survey starts outside the model. One method may estimate success from a single frame; another may predict a percentage from a video, compare two trajectories, or generate executable code. Their goals can be language instructions, images, demonstrations, or simulator predicates, while their outputs range from scores to preferences.\n\nThese choices change what a result means. Temporal ordering tests whether scores follow a sequence, regression tests agreement with an absolute reference, and policy success tests whether a controller can use the signal. Without separating those claims, a smooth reward curve can be mistaken for task grounding, or a strong policy can be mistaken for a faithful progress estimator.\n\nThe interface\n\n## “Good” progress is calibration, ordering, grounding, and uncertainty—not one score\n\nThe taxonomy becomes useful when it changes how results are read. Progress-fidelity tests include scalar calibration against percentages, rubric scores, stages, or hop values; temporal consistency through ordering, rank correlation, or monotonicity; relative progress through pairwise preferences; task grounding under mismatched instructions or counterfactual goals; and answerability or uncertainty when history, viewpoints, or goals are insufficient. A model can pass one and fail another.\n\nRobustness and generalization probe unseen tasks, objects, views, occlusions, distractors, embodiments, failures, reversals, plateaus, retries, and unusual subtask orders. Successful monotonic demonstrations are not enough: they can reward elapsed time rather than genuine advancement.\n\nDownstream utility covers online policy learning, offline relabeling, retrieval and filtering, reranking, planning, and action selection. These tests matter operationally, but they answer a different question. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImagine, Verify, Execute: Memory-guided Agentic Exploration with Vision-Language Models (https://ive-robot.github.io/)\nciteturn16search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; Vision-language models (VLMs), with their semantic reasoning over objects, spatial relations, and potential outcomes, present a compelling foundation for generating high-level exploratory behaviors. ... Exploration is structured around semantic reasoning, verification, and physically grounded interaction. ... The plots show performance across four key metrics as a function of interaction: (Left) the growth in the number of unique scene graphs discovered, (Middle Left) the entropy of visited states (a measure of diversity), (Middle Right) empowerment (the agent's ability to influence future states), and (Right) information gain (the amount of new information acquired).\n# Imagine, Verify, Execute: Memory-guided Agentic Exploration with Vision-Language Models\n\nCoRL 2025\n\nSeungjae Lee^{*} Daniel Ekpo^{*} Haowen Liu\n\nFurong Huang^{‡} Abhinav Shrivastava^{‡} Jia-Bin Huang^{‡}\n\nUniversity of Maryland, College Park\n\n^{*} Equal contribution. ^{†} Equal advising.\n\nPaper arXiv Code\n\nThe IVE (Imagine-Verify-Execute) agent autonomously explores Tangram pieces in the real world (top row), commonobjects (middle row), and objects in simulation (bottom row). Across these tasks, IVE converts visual input to semantic scene graphs, imagines novel configurations, verifies their physical feasibility, and executes actions to gather diverse, semantically-grounded data for downstream learning.\n\n### Abstract\n\nExploration is essential for general-purpose robotic learning, especially in open-ended environments where dense rewards, explicit goals, or task-specific supervision are scarce. Verifier removal slightly degrades performance, particularly in terms of information gain, but empowerment remains relatively stable. The random tool selector baseline performs the worst across all metrics. Our full model (IVE) approaches human-level exploration efficiency.\n\nImage: Ablation baselines\n\nExploring with Embodied Agents. IVE, powered by different Vision-Language Models (VLMs) against a human expert. The plots show performance across four key metrics as a function of interaction: (Left) the growth in the number of unique scene graphs discovered, (Middle Left) the entropy of visited states (a measure of diversity), (Middle Right) empowerment (the agent's ability to influence future states), and (Right) information gain (the amount of new information acquired). Notably, IVE, regardless of the VLM used, surpasses the human expert in generating unique scene graphs, achieving higher state diversity, and gaining more information.\n\nTable 1: Success rates (%) of goal-conditional behavior cloning across tasks in simulation. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProcVLM: Learning Procedure-Grounded Progress Rewards for Robotic Manipulation | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://arxiv.deeppaper.ai/papers/2605.08774v1)\nciteturn16search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The key contribution is a progress-aware vision-language model (ProcVLM) that learns procedure-grounded progress as a dense reward signal for robotic manipulation, along with a large-scale dataset (ProcCorpus-60M) and a standardized procedural supervision synthesis pipeline. ... Rather than deriving progress from terminal outcomes or temporal proxies, ProcVLM grounds progress estimation in procedural structure and intra-stage visual change, and further adopts a reasoning-before-estimation paradigm that infers the remaining atomic actions before estimating task progress. ... Experiments on ProcVQA and reward-model benchmarks show that ProcVLM improves embodied procedural reasoning and yields more discriminative trajectory-internal progress estimates than representative baselines, supporting its use as a dense reward model for downstream reward-guided policy optimization. ... ### arXiv\n\n# ProcVLM: Learning Procedure-Grounded Progress Rewards for Robotic Manipulation\n\nPDF DeepRead\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nThe key contribution is a progress-aware vision-language model (ProcVLM) that learns procedure-grounded progress as a dense reward signal for robotic manipulation, along with a large-scale dataset (ProcCorpus-60M) and a standardized procedural supervision synthesis pipeline.\n\n### Abstract\n\nLong-horizon robotic manipulation requires dense feedback that reflects how a task advances through its procedural stages, not merely whether the final outcome is successful. Existing reward models often rely on trajectory-level success labels or time-based interpolation, which can conflate elapsed time with true task progress and therefore fail to capture unfinished steps, stagnation, and failure states. We present ProcVLM, a progress-aware vision-language model that learns procedure-grounded progress as a dense reward signal for manipulation. Rather than deriving progress from terminal outcomes or temporal proxies, ProcVLM grounds progress estimation in procedural structure and intra-stage visual change, and further adopts a reasoning-before-estimation paradigm that infers the remaining atomic actions before estimating task progress. Specifically, we construct this supervision by synthesizing frame-level subtask-semantic annotations, assigning progress budgets according to subtask structure, and distributing each budget based on intra-subtask visual change. To train ProcVLM at scale, we build a standardized procedural supervision synthesis pipeline and construct ProcCorpus-60M from 30 embodied datasets with 60M annotated frames, from which we derive ProcVQA for procedure-aware pretraining, with progress estimation as the central task alongside action segmentation and future planning. Experiments on ProcVQA and reward-model benchmarks show that ProcVLM improves embodied procedural reasoning and yields more discriminative trajectory-internal progress estimates than representative baselines, supporting its use as a dense reward model for downstream reward-guided policy optimization. Project page: https://procvlm.github.io/\n\n### Summary\n\nProcVLM learns procedure-grounded progress as a dense reward signal for robotic manipulation by grounding progress estimation in procedural structure and intra-stage visual change.\n\n### Subjects\n\ncs.RO cs.LG\n\n### arXiv\n\nImage: arXiv 2605.08774v1\n\n### Paper Type\n\nmethod Dataset application framework\n\n### Domain\n\nAI Robotics Manipulation\n\nAuthors\n\nYouhe Feng, Hansen Shi, Haoyang Li, Xinlei Guo, Yang Wang, Chengyang Zhang, Jinkai Zhang, Xiaohan Zhang, Jie Tang, Jing Zhang\n\nSubmitted\n\nMay 09, 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nfor exploration [11], while goal-conditioned reward functions (e.g. LIV [12]) often rely on global scene-level embeddings that miss the fine-grained, incremental progress essential for OSC. Together, these limitations make current approaches sample-inefficient and ill-suited to tasks where state changes unfold dynamically within the object. (https://vision.cs.utexas.edu/projects/sparta-robot/SPARTA.pdf)\nciteturn16search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; SPARTA leverages SPOC affordance² maps in two crucial ways: (1) as structured visual observations that strip away appearance detail while preserving progress cues, enabling generalization across objects; and (2) as dense, spatially grounded reward signals that quantify incremental progress at each step. ... By contrast, baseline methods fail to learn meaningful behavior, highlighting that dense, interpretable affordances for object state change are key to enabling sample-efficient, generalizable real-world robot learning—charting a path beyond rigid-body manipulation. ... Understanding how and where to interact with objects has driven a surge of interest in affordance-based functional grasping [26–31]. ... By integrating vision-based OSC understanding into robot manipulation, we show how robots can learn to act using SPOC-style affordances capturing gradual visual progress—difficult to address with tactile sensing [19], force models, or binary state classifiers [2, 39].\nfor exploration [11], while goal-conditioned reward functions (e.g. LIV [12]) often rely on global scene-level embeddings that miss the fine-grained, incremental progress essential for OSC. Together, these limitations make current approaches sample-inefficient and ill-suited to tasks where state changes unfold dynamically within the object.\n\nTo tackle these challenges, we propose SPARTA (Spatial Progress-Aware Robotic object TransformAtion)—a robotic system that introduces structured, progress-aware visual affordances tailored to OSC manipulation (see Fig. 1, bottom). SPARTA builds on recent computer vision advances in detecting spatially-progressing object state changes (SPOC [8]), integrating them into a fully autonomous robotic system. SPOC segments a transforming object into two regions: actionable and transformed. For instance, in mashing a potato, unmashed chunks are actionable, while mashed portions are transformed. SPARTA leverages SPOC affordance² maps in two crucial ways: (1) as structured visual observations that strip away appearance detail while preserving progress cues, enabling generalization across objects; and (2) as dense, spatially grounded reward signals that quantify incremental progress at each step. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Structured Hierarchical Model for Human-Robot (https://media-api.suub.uni-bremen.de/api/core/bitstreams/bb8306b0-f3c4-486f-bb14-63053b929c2e/content)\nciteturn16search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; subject, such as a robot changing its end-effector. ... classified task progression into functional state changes and interaction state ... Functional state changes describe shifts in the role or purpose of an\n3 State of the art\n(Zhang, Wang, et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022). By recognizing the objects\ninvolved in each action, the system provides a more detailed understanding of task\nexecution beyond human movement alone. This automated approach simplifies\ntask monitoring and reduces manual effort. However, it remains limited to a\npredefined set of task labels and cannot provide detailed insights into sub-\nprocesses within each task.\nTo formalize the principles underlying task monitoring, recent studies have\nintroduced the concept of relationships between manipulating subjects and\nmanipulated objects (Fitzgerald et al., 2021; Lee et al., 2024). Fitzgerald et al.\n(